@** XR output. Everything so far draws on a monitor. This part sends the same
ray-traced scene to a head-mounted display, one image per eye, through
\emph{OpenXR}, the Khronos standard that sits between an application and the
\emph{runtime} of a headset. The runtime we have in mind is NVIDIA CloudXR,
which does not drive a headset cable at all: it encodes the eye images as
video and streams them over the network to an Apple Vision Pro or a Meta
Quest, which send their head poses back. To the application CloudXR is an
ordinary OpenXR runtime, so nothing below is specific to it except the small
class that can start it inside our own process.

Two classes do the work. [[CloudXrService]] (in \lpfile{CloudXrService.h}
and \lpfile{CloudXrService.cpp}) loads the CloudXR runtime from a CloudXR SDK
and starts it, so that a single command brings up both the viewer and the
streaming server. [[XrOutput]] (in \lpfile{XrOutput.h} and
\lpfile{XrOutput.cpp}) is the OpenXR client proper: it finds the headset,
creates the session, and every frame hands the application the eye cameras
and two buffers to render into, then passes the finished images to the
runtime. The application, in the previous part, decides when to call it.

The guiding constraint is that the eye images must not cross the PCI bus. The
renderer produces them in GPU memory with CUDA; the runtime wants them in
Vulkan images. Rather than downloading the pixels to the host and uploading
them again---some 35~MB per frame for two eyes of 2400 by 1920 pixels, ninety
times a second---the eye buffers are allocated by Vulkan, \emph{exported} to
CUDA, and written by OptiX directly. A copy on the GPU then moves each buffer
into the image the runtime gave us.

@* The CloudXR service. CloudXR comes as a set of shared libraries. The OpenXR runtime, which the
OpenXR loader opens when an application creates an instance, is one of them:
\lpfile{libopenxr\_cloudxr.so}. It does not do the
streaming itself: it talks, through a local pipe, to the CloudXR
\emph{service}, which owns the network connection to the headset and the
video encoder. The service lives in \lpfile{libcloudxr.so} and is driven by a
small C API, \lpfile{cxrServiceAPI.h}, that the SDK ships under a permissive
license; we keep a copy of the header among the third-party sources.

The service can run in a process of its own (NVIDIA's Isaac Teleop starts it
that way, and then any OpenXR application on the machine can use it), or in
the application's process, which is what [[CloudXrService]] does. Either way
it must be running \emph{before} the OpenXR instance is created, because
creating the instance is when the runtime library connects to it. The class
therefore sits apart from [[XrOutput]], and the application starts it first.

