@ An SDK directory is recognized by its OpenXR manifest,
\lpfile{openxr\_cloudxr.json}, which we shall later hand to the loader. The
service library depends on several others in the same directory (the
streaming engine, a private OpenSSL, a CUDA runtime); its \emph{run path} is
[[$ORIGIN]], so loading it by its full path is enough for the dynamic linker
to find them all. [[RTLD_LOCAL]] keeps its symbols out of the global
namespace: the SDK's CUDA runtime, version 12, must not be confused with the
version 13 runtime that the renderer is linked against.

<<Find and load the CloudXR library>>=
const std::filesystem::path dir(sdkDir);
const std::filesystem::path manifest = dir / "openxr_cloudxr.json";
if (!std::filesystem::exists(manifest))
{
    error = "No CloudXR SDK in " + sdkDir + " (openxr_cloudxr.json not found)";
    return false;
}
// the library finds its own dependencies next to it (RUNPATH $ORIGIN)
m_library = dlopen((dir / "libcloudxr.so").c_str(), RTLD_NOW | RTLD_LOCAL);
if (!m_library)
{
    error = std::string("Could not load libcloudxr.so: ") + dlerror();
    return false;
}
@ The generic lambda [[resolve]] looks a symbol up and casts it to the type
of the pointer it is given, which saves repeating every function type. Once
the functions are known we ask for the runtime's version; the XR menu shows
it, and its major number is the streaming protocol a client must speak.

<<Resolve the entry points of the CloudXR service>>=
auto resolve = [&](auto& fn, const char* name) {
    fn = reinterpret_cast<std::remove_reference_t<decltype(fn)>>(dlsym(m_library, name));
    if (!fn)
        error = std::string("libcloudxr.so lacks ") + name;
    return fn != nullptr;
};
if (!resolve(m_getRuntimeVersion, "nv_cxr_get_runtime_version") || !resolve(m_create, "nv_cxr_service_create") ||
    !resolve(m_setStringProperty, "nv_cxr_service_set_string_property") ||
    !resolve(m_start, "nv_cxr_service_start") || !resolve(m_stop, "nv_cxr_service_stop") ||
    !resolve(m_join, "nv_cxr_service_join") || !resolve(m_destroy, "nv_cxr_service_destroy") ||
    !resolve(m_pollEvent, "nv_cxr_service_poll_event"))
{
    stop();
    return false;
}
uint32_t major = 0, minor = 0, patch = 0;
m_getRuntimeVersion(&major, &minor, &patch);
m_version = std::to_string(major) + "." + std::to_string(minor) + "." + std::to_string(patch);
@ Properties can only be set between creating and starting the service. The
only one we set is the device profile, and only when the user named one.

<<Create the CloudXR service and choose its device profile>>=
nv_cxr_result_t result = m_create(&m_service);
if (result == NV_CXR_SUCCESS && !deviceProfile.empty())
{
    const std::string name = "device-profile";
    result = m_setStringProperty(m_service, name.data(), name.size(), deviceProfile.data(), deviceProfile.size());
}
@ Starting the service has a side effect that took some finding. The
service, like the Monado runtime it is built on, installs handlers for
[[SIGINT]] and [[SIGTERM]] that stop it. In a process of its own that is
right; in ours it is not, because a signal would pull the runtime away while
our session is still submitting frames, the next OpenXR call would fail, and
SDL, whose own handlers were replaced, would never learn that it should quit.
So we remember the handlers that were in place---SDL's, which turn the
signals into a quit event---and put them back as soon as the service has
started. A [[SIGTERM]] then takes the normal way out: the main loop ends, the
session is destroyed, and only then the service stops.

<<Start the CloudXR service, keeping our signal handlers>>=
if (result == NV_CXR_SUCCESS)
{
    // The service installs SIGINT/SIGTERM handlers that stop it while the
    // app still uses it. Give the signals back to SDL, whose quit event
    // ends the session first and then stops the service.
    struct sigaction interrupt{}, terminate{};
    sigaction(SIGINT, nullptr, &interrupt);
    sigaction(SIGTERM, nullptr, &terminate);
    result = m_start(m_service);
    sigaction(SIGINT, &interrupt, nullptr);
    sigaction(SIGTERM, &terminate, nullptr);
}
@ The usual reason for a failed start is another CloudXR runtime already
running on the machine, since only one service can hold the network ports.
The embedded service writes its log to the console, which is where the
message sends the user. On success, [[XR_RUNTIME_JSON]] is pointed at the
SDK's manifest; the OpenXR loader reads this variable when the instance is
created, and so the instance will be served by the CloudXR runtime library,
which in turn connects to the service we have just started.

<<Report a failed CloudXR start, or point OpenXR at the runtime>>=
if (result != NV_CXR_SUCCESS)
{
    error = "The CloudXR service did not start (error " + std::to_string(int(result)) +
            "). Is another CloudXR runtime running? The service's messages are on the console.";
    if (m_service)
    {
        m_destroy(m_service);
        m_service = nullptr;
    }
    stop();
    return false;
}
// the OpenXR loader reads this when the instance is created
setenv("XR_RUNTIME_JSON", manifest.c_str(), 1);
return true;
@ Stopping is [[stop]] followed by [[join]], which waits until the service
has really ended, and then destroying it. [[stop]] is safe to call at any
time, also on a half-started service.

<<Stop the CloudXR service>>=
void CloudXrService::stop()
{
    if (m_service)
    {
        m_stop(m_service);
        m_join(m_service);
        m_destroy(m_service);
        m_service = nullptr;
    }
    if (m_library)
    {
        dlclose(m_library);
        m_library = nullptr;
    }
    m_clientConnected = false;
}
@ The service queues events: a client (the headset) connected or
disconnected, an OpenXR application connected or disconnected. Only the
first pair interests us, for the status shown in the XR menu. One oddity of
the API is that ``no event'' is reported as a successful call that returns an
event of type [[NV_CXR_EVENT_NONE]], so the loop must test the type as well
as the result.

<<Poll the events of the CloudXR service>>=
bool CloudXrService::clientConnected()
{
    if (!m_service)
        return false;
    // poll_event reports "no event" as a success with type NV_CXR_EVENT_NONE
    nv_cxr_event_t event{};
    while (m_pollEvent(m_service, &event) == NV_CXR_SUCCESS && event.type != NV_CXR_EVENT_NONE)
    {
        if (event.type == NV_CXR_EVENT_CLOUDXR_CLIENT_CONNECTED)
            m_clientConnected = true;
        else if (event.type == NV_CXR_EVENT_CLOUDXR_CLIENT_DISCONNECTED)
            m_clientConnected = false;
        event = {};
    }
    return m_clientConnected;
}
