@ The header pulls in the CUDA runtime (for the external-memory handle of
each eye), the Vulkan and OpenXR headers, and the shared launch parameters,
whose [[Camera]] the eye cameras are expressed in. The build defines
[[VK_NO_PROTOTYPES]], so the Vulkan header declares types but no functions,
and [[XR_USE_GRAPHICS_API_VULKAN]], which makes \lpfile{openxr\_platform.h}
declare the Vulkan binding of OpenXR.

<<Opening of the XR output header>>=
// Stereo output through OpenXR, for example to a headset streamed by NVIDIA
// CloudXR. The runtime chooses the GPU and creates the Vulkan device. Each
// eye has a buffer in Vulkan memory that is shared with CUDA, so OptiX renders
// straight into it; a copy on the Vulkan queue moves it into the eye's
// swapchain image.
#pragma once

#include <array>
#include <chrono>
#include <string>
#include <vector>

#include <cuda_runtime.h>
#include <vulkan/vulkan.h>
#include <openxr/openxr.h>
#include <openxr/openxr_platform.h>

#include "LaunchParams.h"
@ The states, in the order they are normally visited. Without an instance
the output is [[Off]]. With one, it looks for an OpenXR \emph{system}---a
headset---and stays [[WaitingForSystem]] until there is one. A static
CloudXR profile has a system at once; an [[auto-*]] profile only after a
client has connected, which may take minutes, and the desktop must remain
usable meanwhile. Once the system is there and the Vulkan device has been
made, the output is [[SystemReady]] and waits for the application to create
the session; the application has a job to do first, as we shall see. The
session then sits [[Idle]] until the runtime declares it ready, and is
[[Running]] while frames flow.

<<States of the XR output>>=
enum class State
{
    Off,               // no OpenXR instance
    WaitingForSystem,  // no headset yet (a CloudXR "auto" profile waits for a client)
    SystemReady,       // Vulkan device created; the app must call createSession()
    Idle,              // session created, not running
    Running,           // frames are being submitted
};
@ The headset reports the eyes in its own coordinates, in meters, relative
to where the head was when the session began. Something must say where that
origin is in the scene. The [[Anchor]] does: an eye point, a direction to
face, which axis is up, and a scale. The application fills it from the
desktop view, so that the user starts where the desktop camera is.

<<Placing the XR user in the scene>>=
// Places the user in the scene: the origin of the XR space (the head's
// position when the session started) goes to `eye`, facing the
// horizontal part of `forward`, and one meter spans unitsPerMeter units.
struct Anchor
{
    float eye[3];
    float forward[3];
    bool zUp;
    float unitsPerMeter;
};
@ The life cycle in calls: [[init]] creates the instance, [[update]] is
called every main-loop turn, and when the state is [[SystemReady]] the
application makes
CUDA current on the GPU identified by [[deviceUuid]] before calling
[[createSession]]. The UUID is the only reliable way to name a GPU across
APIs: CUDA and Vulkan number the two GPUs of this machine independently.

<<Construction and session control of the XR output>>=
XrOutput() = default;
~XrOutput();
XrOutput(const XrOutput&) = delete;
XrOutput& operator=(const XrOutput&) = delete;

// Creates the OpenXR instance with the active runtime (XR_RUNTIME_JSON).
bool init(std::string& error);
// Ends the session and releases everything (State::Off).
void stop();
// Handles runtime events and looks for the headset. On an error the XR
// output is shut down (State::Off) and `error` is set.
State update(std::string& error);
State state() const { return m_state; }
// In State::SystemReady: CUDA must be current on the GPU with this UUID
// before createSession(), which shares memory between Vulkan and CUDA.
const std::array<unsigned char, 16>& deviceUuid() const { return m_deviceUuid; }
bool createSession(float resolutionScale, std::string& error);
